{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n1le01ob11re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n1le01ob11re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob11re2img01.jpg", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Teacher</b>: Guys, today we are reviewing the 4 basic concepts of object-oriented programming. To start with, does anybody know how to implement them?<br /> <b>Sarah</b>: As far as I&rsquo;m concerned, we implement those concepts by using classes, methods, and objects, right?&nbsp;<br /> <b>Teacher</b>: You're right, but that&rsquo;s a topic we are covering later on. Before that, can you tell us what the four core concepts of object-oriented programming are?&nbsp;<br /> <b>Sarah</b>: Yes, I know they are abstraction, encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism. Though I don't understand the difference among them.<br /> <b>Teacher</b>: Don't worry, we need to take our time to understand those concepts. Let's start with abstraction. This is useful for programmers since it conceals the less important details of an object.</p>", "time": "0"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob11re2img02.jpg", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Sarah</b>: It means it doesn't show the whole information but just the more relevant details?<br /> <b>Teacher</b>: Exactly! Of course, it eases the process and makes it more efficient. Encapsulation, on the other hand, keeps data away from danger, as it doesn't allow any misuse or interference.&nbsp;<br /> <b>Sarah</b>: How does it keep it safe?<br /> <b>Teacher</b>: It hides the internal mechanism and data structures, which means it doesn't endorse any arbitrary access. Besides, it behaves as a wrapper that covers the code and data. So, are these two concepts clear so far?<br /> <b>Sarah</b>: Yes, you can continue.&nbsp;<br /> <b>Teacher</b>: Great. Now, let's talk about inheritance and polymorphism. Inheritance is a process that creates new classes from existing ones.</p>", "time": "54300"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob11re2img03.jpg", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Sarah</b>: Do these new classes inherit something from the predecessor ones?<br /> <b>Teacher</b>: Yes! They inherit certain characteristics. Anyway, we are going to cover that topic later. Let's continue with polymorphism.&nbsp;<br /> <b>Sarah</b>: Sorry, professor. What does that mean?<br /> <b>Teacher</b>: Basically, it means that something has one name and at the same time multiple forms. Talking about programming, it states that all properties of an object are susceptible to change their form according to a certain context.</p>", "time": "104000"}]}}], "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can list the basic concepts in object oriented programming.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can mention characteristics for abstraction and encapsulation in object oriented programming. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about characteristics for inheritance and polymorphism in object oriented programming.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Do you think OOP is easy to use? Why?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "What is a paradigm in programming? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"conten