s of the program doesn't require the abstraction concept.   ", "type": "text"}], "type": "question"}, {"content": "secondary_1", "type": "objective"}]}, {"id": 2, "responses": [{"selected": 0, "id": 0, "correct": 1, "_data": [{"content": "true", "type": "text"}]}, {"selected": 0, "id": 1, "correct": 0, "_data": [{"content": "false", "type": "text"}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "The encapsulation concept doesn't create new programs.   ", "type": "text"}], "type": "question"}, {"content": "secondary_1", "type": "objective"}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "Abstraction or Encapsulation?", "type": "text"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Quiz", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: <span class=\"bullet\">Listen to the audio. </span> <span class=\"bullet\">Click on TRUE if the statement is true.</span> <span class=\"bullet\">Click on FALSE if the statement is false. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11re6aud08", "type": "audio"}], "type": "post_instructions"}]}, {"id": 11, "type": "RSLT", "stimuli_ids": [], "template_override": 1, "questions": [{"id": 0, "responses": [{"selected": 0, "id": 0, "correct": 0, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, {"selected": 0, "id": 1, "correct": 1, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "type": "question"}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Quiz", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!"}, "type": "feedback"}]}, {"id": 12, "type": "DWS", "stimuli_ids": [], "template_override": 1, "questions": [{"id": 0, "responses": [{"selected": 0, "id": 0, "correct": 1, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11re7pdf01.pdf", "type": "pdf"}], "type": "question"}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "What can you say about concepts in OOP?", "type": "text"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Take Away", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}]}], "type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Concepts in Object Oriented Programming", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 11, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about object oriented programming.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Identify and explain the characteristics of software.  Discuss information about designing principles in software programming", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands key information in simple written and spoken texts about topics related to software programming and its history.  Recognizes basic ideas on programming concepts and programming history in both written and spoken passages.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Demonstratives as Pronouns, Adjectives, in Plural Forms", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Demonstratives</b> are words we use to refer to the position of an object in relation to us, the speakers.</p>", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">We can divide demonstratives into&nbsp;<b>demonstrative adjectives </b>and&nbsp;<b>demonstrative pronouns.</b>&nbsp;<br /> <br />The demonstrative pronouns are <b>this</b>&nbsp;(near) and&nbsp;<b>that</b>&nbsp;(far), for singular nouns;<b>&nbsp;these</b>&nbsp;(near) and&nbsp;<b>those</b>&nbsp;(far), for plural nouns. They represent and identify an object, and they can stand alone.&nbsp;<br /> <br />For example:&nbsp;</p> <ul style=\"text-align: justify;\"> <li><i>I can't believe&nbsp;<b>this</b>!</i></li> <li><b><i>This&nbsp;</i></b><i>sounds like a good idea!</i><br /> <br />Demonstrative adjectives, on the other hand, modify nouns, which means they can't stand alone, they need a noun.&nbsp;<br /> <br />For example:&nbsp;</li> <li><b><i>These codes&nbsp;</i></b><i>are necessary for programming. </i><i>(<b>These</b>&nbsp;modifies&nbsp;<b>codes</b>&nbsp;)&nbsp;</i></li> </ul> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Observe the following chart to remember demonstratives use:</p>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Let's start with abstraction. This is useful for programmers since it conceals the less important details of an object.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You're right, but that's a topic we are covering later on.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Near the speaker<strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Far from the speaker<strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Singular<strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"This\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"That\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Plural<strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"These\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Those\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}", "type": "table"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Simple Present Tense in Negative Statements", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">You use the <b>Simple Present Tense </b>to talk about habits and general facts.</p>", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">We use the present simple tense to express routines and general facts that happen on a regular basis.</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In order to express a negative idea in the present simple tense, use the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b>Subject+ auxiliary verb (do/ does) + not+ main verb in base form+ complement.</b><br /> <br />For example:&nbsp;</p> <ul style=\"text-align: justify;\"> <li><i>I&nbsp;<b>don't understand</b> that programming concept.&nbsp;</i><br /> </li> <li><i>Jane&nbsp;<b>doesn't know </b>what OOP stands for.</i><br /> </li> <li><i>The software designer of the company <b>doesn't like </b>to use open source programs to code.&nbsp;</i></li> </ul> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As you can see, you keep the verb in its base form, since we include the auxiliary in the sentence. Also, note that the auxiliary verb <b>do</b> changes to <b>does </b>when you use it with a third person in singular form (he, she, and it).</p>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I know they are abstraction, encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism. Though I don't understand the difference between them.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Excuse me. Does It mean it doesn't show the whole information but just the more relevant details?", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "conceal", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to hide", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Let's start with abstraction. This is useful for programmers since it conceals the less important details of an object.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "ease", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to make something easy", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: it eases the process and makes it more efficient. Encapsulation, on the other hand, keeps data away from danger.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "misuse", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the incorrect use of something ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Encapsulation, on the other hand, keeps data away from danger, as it doesn't allow any misuse or interference. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "inherit", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to take or receive things or characteristics from a predecessor", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Do these new classes inherit something from the predecessor ones?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "endorse ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to approve or sustain ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It hides the internal mechanism and data structures, which means it doesn't endorse any arbitrary access.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "wrapper", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something that covers or protect certain item ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Besides, it behaves as a wrapper that covers the code and data. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob11aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Look for more information about OOP concepts.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write four steps about how to implement OOP concepts.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Use the demonstratives as adjectives and pronouns in your sentences.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Show the steps to your teacher. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I look for additional information about OOP concepts.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write four steps about how to implement OOP concepts.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I use demonstratives as adjectives and pronouns in my sentences", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}]}